;;; A dead node's core leaves out the activator its refutation rests on, where
;;; the activator is the commitment itself — M1e2 **W19**, register row
;;; `W19/core-activator-committed` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.19 (2026-11-04), found by probing `forbid-chain-no-absent.ein`'s
;;; mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two candidates, `(r2 a)` and `(p2 a)`. `(r2 a)` is an activator: the
;;; plan of `r2` it compiles concludes `(false)` from `(seed a)`. Root holds
;;; neither candidate, so it compiles no plan of `r2`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is two states, ∅ and {(p2 a)}; N is {(r2 a)}. Its refutation rests on
;;; `(seed a)`, which the plan matched, and on `(r2 a)`, the activator the
;;; plan was compiled from: the core is `(r2 a) (seed a)`, which names the
;;; commitment. `(seed a)` alone is root's, and root is alive.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what holds since ─────────────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.19, R as above, exit 0; and N's core `(seed a)` alone, by
;;; `r2`, in `--dump-answer`'s `nogoods.json`, `--dump-lattice`'s
;;; `nodes.json`, `--events`' `enter` line, `--trace` and `ein oracle
;;; --dump` (`ein solve FILE --dump-answer DIR` at `a41eb76e`, release and
;;; debug). A derivation recorded the premises its plan matched, not the
;;; activator the plan was compiled from, so the core search never reached
;;; the commitment. Which
;;; activators a core follows was Q-M1e2.12, S1e2.2.19's question: read as
;;; the stage file's narrower reading is worded, *only an activator that is
;;; itself derived*, this one, a commitment, would still be left out. The
;;; user ruled (c) on 2026-11-04, an activator a node derives or commits, and
;;; since S1e2.2.19's fix N's core is `(r2 a) (seed a)`, in either build.

(relation seed T :why "{?1} is a seed") (relation r2 T :why "r2 holds of {?1}, which activates rule r2 for {?1}") (relation p2 T :why "p2 holds of {?1}")

(seed a)

(hrule g  () :match (seed ?x) :assert (r2 ?x) :why "guess that r2 holds of the seed {?x}")
(hrule g2 () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p2 ?x) :why "guess that p2 holds of the seed {?x}")

(rule r2 (?k) :match (seed ?k) :assert (false) :why "rule r2 is activated for {?k}, a seed, which cannot be")
